when working on machine learning  problems it's easy to be laser focused  on the model training  but the data is really where it all  starts so today we'll cover the basics  of storing data for model training and  see how to access training data from the  vertex ai workbench notebook that we  created in the last episode i'm nikita  and this is prototype to production  where we go from notebook code to a  deployed model in the cloud  [Music]  before we get into the data let's take a  look at the image classification example  that we'll use throughout the series but  note that if you have a different data  type or prediction task a lot of what we  cover in this series will still be  applicable to your use case  so here's a collab notebook that trains  an image classification model on the  flower photos data set  the data set is in a folder called  flower photos which gets loaded directly  into the runtime when i execute this  cell  the flower photos directory contains  five subdirectories one for each flower  type in our data set  these directories contain jpegs of the  corresponding flowers  the next cells in our notebook create  our training and validation data sets  define the model using tensorflow and  keras then train and save the model  our goal is to take all of this code and  run it on google cloud's manage machine  learning platform vertex ai  but if you want to train models on  vertex ai first you need to get your  data into the cloud if you've been  working in a different environment like  colab or locally etc you can upload any  code artifacts you need to your vertex  ci workbench managed notebook and you  can even integrate with github  in the future you can do all of your  development right here in workbench but  for now let's download this collab  notebook as a ipynb file and then upload  it to workbench  if you're using something like tf  datasets or downloading data off the  internet you can do that directly in the  notebook just like you would in collab  or in a local environment  so for example if i execute these cells  here it'll download the flowers dataset  directly to the instance and we can  access them that way  or if your dataset is small enough you  could even load it directly into the  instance by clicking the upload button  right here  now that we've downloaded the data we  can run through the rest of the cells in  this notebook  and there you go you're training a model  on google cloud  now this might work for experimentation  but for something more production ready  you probably want to store the data  within your project in a centralized  location instead of directly on the  machine where your notebook is running  and that's what cloud storage is there  for cloud storage is google cloud's  object storage  meaning you can store images csv files  text files saved model artifacts just  about anything  cloud storage has the concept of a  bucket which is what holds your data  everything you store in cloud storage  must be contained in a bucket and within  a bucket you can create folders to  organize your data  each file in cloud storage has a path  just like a file on your local file  system except that cloud storage paths  always start with gs colon slash so here  in the ui i've created a bucket called  prototype to production bucket  you can upload files and create folders  in the ui  or you can upload files programmatically  with the gsutil tool  this tool is installed automatically in  workbench but if you want to use it to  locally upload data from your machine to  cloud storage then you'll need to  install the cloud sdk and instructions  on how to do that are linked below  so let's use the gsutil command to copy  the data we downloaded in our workbench  notebook to our cloud storage bucket  we add the exclamation point to execute  this as a shell command then type gsutil  cp to copy  we add the dash r because we want to  copy the entire directory and dash m to  perform a multi-processing copy which  will just speed things up  once the data is copied we can check out  the bucket in the ui  we can go into one of these folders and  see all of the images  when you have data in cloud storage you  can often still access it using the same  methods you would before  you'll just need to pass in the cloud  storage path also known as a uri instead  of a local path  so for example if you're familiar with  using tf records you might use the tf  data tf record data set class to create  your data set you can pass in a cloud  storage uri  and read the data just like you would if  the data was stored locally  and you could even do the same thing  with image dataset from directory which  is the function we're using in this  sample in this episode we saw how to  download data directly to our vertex ai  workbench instances for experimentation  and how to create and upload data to  cloud storage buckets via the ui and the  gsutil tool  vertex ai workbench and cloud storage  are building blocks  that will be a core part of our machine  learning journey on google cloud in the  next episode we'll learn about running  custom training jobs on vertex ai's  managed training service and how to kick  off a training job from a workbench  instance don't forget to like and  subscribe for more great content and  i'll see you next time  [Music]  you
 

